1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:06,643
We stopped one container, the frontend,
and restarted the web server in front of it.

2
00:00:06,963 --> 00:00:11,293
The API container is still up,
and so is the vector database.

3
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:15,800
Yet the whole site answers nothing,
the address of the API included.

4
00:00:16,033 --> 00:00:20,534
Today's puzzle: the API is running,
so why is its address dead too?

5
00:00:20,854 --> 00:00:23,543
We answer it once
we have built this by hand.

6
00:00:23,783 --> 00:00:27,371
Here is the goal,
the lecture's picture of deployment:

7
00:00:27,371 --> 00:00:32,421
the containers from your laptop move
to one virtual machine in Google Cloud,

8
00:00:32,421 --> 00:00:34,215
with port 80 the only door.

9
00:00:34,535 --> 00:00:37,138
We build it twice: by hand, then as code.

10
00:00:37,383 --> 00:00:42,354
On the course roadmap, the marker has moved
to deployment, scaling and automation.

11
00:00:42,674 --> 00:00:45,362
Everything so far ran on your own computer.

12
00:00:45,682 --> 00:00:47,133
Users need a server.

13
00:00:47,367 --> 00:00:51,162
You need cheese-app-v3
with your secrets folder beside it,

14
00:00:51,162 --> 00:00:55,218
a Google Cloud project with billing,
and a Docker Hub account.

15
00:00:55,538 --> 00:00:59,825
This costs real money,
so each part ends by deleting everything.

16
00:01:00,067 --> 00:01:03,184
The tracker in the corner
names the README step,

17
00:01:03,184 --> 00:01:05,651
code panels name their file and lines,

18
00:01:05,651 --> 00:01:09,027
and a yellow tag,
your turn, means pause and run it.

19
00:01:09,267 --> 00:01:10,352
One honest note:

20
00:01:10,352 --> 00:01:14,963
our recording machine has no Google account
and no Docker Hub login.

21
00:01:15,283 --> 00:01:17,822
What runs on a machine you own is real;

22
00:01:17,822 --> 00:01:21,011
every cloud step stops
at its first honest error,

23
00:01:21,011 --> 00:01:22,899
and we say what yours prints.

24
00:01:24,033 --> 00:01:25,697
Let's draw the goal properly.

25
00:01:26,017 --> 00:01:31,297
A browser sends a request to port 80,
where nginx passes pages

26
00:01:31,297 --> 00:01:36,662
to the frontend on port 3000
and data calls to the API on 9000.

27
00:01:36,982 --> 00:01:40,502
The API asks the vector database on 8000.

28
00:01:40,750 --> 00:01:43,443
The slide draws
its arrowheads the other way;

29
00:01:43,443 --> 00:01:45,657
we draw requests the way they travel.

30
00:01:45,977 --> 00:01:47,982
The slide shows four containers.

31
00:01:48,302 --> 00:01:49,367
There is a fifth:

32
00:01:49,367 --> 00:01:53,187
a loader that fills the vector database once
and then leaves.

33
00:01:53,433 --> 00:01:58,151
The lecture splits the manual route
into four stages: push the images,

34
00:01:58,151 --> 00:02:02,734
set up the VM, deploy the containers,
and put a web server in front.

35
00:02:03,054 --> 00:02:04,868
That is our map for part 1.

36
00:02:05,116 --> 00:02:09,724
The README starts with a check,
not a command: your containers run locally.

37
00:02:10,044 --> 00:02:15,197
The vector database is up,
the API shows its docs on port 9000,

38
00:02:15,197 --> 00:02:18,386
and the frontend its home page on 3000.

39
00:02:18,633 --> 00:02:24,329
A small note: on this code, /status
is a better API check than the docs page.

40
00:02:24,649 --> 00:02:28,532
It answers version 3.0,
so you know which code runs.

41
00:02:28,766 --> 00:02:34,035
If you followed lecture 18 lately,
it now teaches a refactored version 2;

42
00:02:34,035 --> 00:02:38,004
here you run version 3,
with this repository's scripts.

43
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:41,893
We follow main,
the branch the course site links;

44
00:02:41,893 --> 00:02:45,610
on recording day it was this
November 2025 commit.

45
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:49,066
READMEs change;
git log shows which one you have.

46
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,248
Stage 1: push the images.

47
00:02:52,568 --> 00:02:56,249
Create an access token
in your Docker Hub account settings;

48
00:02:56,249 --> 00:02:59,181
it is a password for scripts,
so never show it.

49
00:02:59,417 --> 00:03:03,336
Typed exactly as the README prints it,
the login fails:

50
00:03:03,336 --> 00:03:06,542
syntax error near
unexpected token `newline'.

51
00:03:06,862 --> 00:03:09,712
Angle brackets
are redirections to the shell,

52
00:03:09,712 --> 00:03:13,006
so replace the whole placeholder,
brackets included.

53
00:03:13,250 --> 00:03:17,666
Better: the flag --password-stdin keeps
the token out of your shell history.

54
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:21,207
Now the Docker Hub script,
one in each service folder.

55
00:03:21,527 --> 00:03:27,159
Line 11 sets the user name to dlops,
the course account: change it to yours.

56
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:30,925
Line 27 creates
and selects a new BuildKit builder;

57
00:03:30,925 --> 00:03:34,035
the script builds,
then builds again to push.

58
00:03:34,355 --> 00:03:42,803
The API script builds for AMD64 and ARM64;
the frontend script, AMD64 only.

59
00:03:43,050 --> 00:03:46,805
Inside frontend-react,
outside any docker shell, run it.

60
00:03:47,125 --> 00:03:51,690
It prints dlops, creates the builder,
and builds: Compiled successfully.

61
00:03:52,010 --> 00:03:54,719
The warning says this build
stays in the builder cache.

62
00:03:54,967 --> 00:03:56,972
Then the push: access denied.

63
00:03:57,292 --> 00:03:59,588
We are not dlops, and not logged in;

64
00:03:59,588 --> 00:04:03,095
with your name and login,
this is where it says pushed.

65
00:04:03,333 --> 00:04:06,703
Run the same script
in api-service and vector-db;

66
00:04:06,703 --> 00:04:09,797
we did not capture
those two-platform builds.

67
00:04:10,117 --> 00:04:14,823
Those two build for two chip types,
one of them emulated:

68
00:04:14,823 --> 00:04:20,272
expect a long api-service build,
and on Linux register QEMU first.

69
00:04:20,592 --> 00:04:23,301
Pause, run all three, and check Docker Hub.

70
00:04:23,550 --> 00:04:26,237
Skip this stage and part 1 still works,

71
00:04:26,237 --> 00:04:31,059
using the public dlops images,
which are a little older than the code.

72
00:04:31,379 --> 00:04:36,201
If you pushed yours, replace dlops
with your user name in the VM commands.

73
00:04:37,350 --> 00:04:39,441
Stage 2: set up the VM.

74
00:04:39,761 --> 00:04:44,755
In the console,
create a VM instance with the defaults,

75
00:04:44,755 --> 00:04:50,747
except machine type N2D, a 50 GB disk,
and HTTP and HTTPS allowed.

76
00:04:50,983 --> 00:04:56,851
Those two ticks add
network tags, http-server and https-server;

77
00:04:56,851 --> 00:05:02,439
the default firewall
opens 80 and 443 only to VMs with them.

78
00:05:02,759 --> 00:05:06,557
The VM now bills while it runs,
its disk while it exists.

79
00:05:06,800 --> 00:05:08,344
Pause, create yours,

80
00:05:08,344 --> 00:05:12,667
and SSH in with the SSH button
in the VM instances list.

81
00:05:12,987 --> 00:05:16,599
Our VM is a stand-in
we can capture end to end:

82
00:05:16,599 --> 00:05:19,749
a fresh Debian 12 machine without Docker.

83
00:05:20,069 --> 00:05:22,352
Every command is the same on yours.

84
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:27,677
Install Docker with the official script:
download it with curl, run it with sudo.

85
00:05:27,997 --> 00:05:33,821
It adds the Docker repository for bookworm
and installs the engine, buildx and compose.

86
00:05:34,633 --> 00:05:37,812
Then check it with sudo docker --version.

87
00:05:38,132 --> 00:05:40,841
Pause here and install Docker on your VM.

88
00:05:41,083 --> 00:05:42,729
Next, create folders:

89
00:05:42,729 --> 00:05:47,590
persistent-folder, secrets and conf/nginx,
writable by anyone.

90
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:52,966
The README lists these six commands twice:
run them once, from your home directory.

91
00:05:55,667 --> 00:05:58,699
An extra check: the folders belong to root,

92
00:05:58,699 --> 00:06:02,366
but anyone may write there:
fine for a demo VM only.

93
00:06:02,686 --> 00:06:06,461
Stay in your home directory:
later mounts use the current folder.

94
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:11,151
Now the app's key,
gcp-service.json, written with echo;

95
00:06:11,151 --> 00:06:13,740
ours holds just the placeholder.

96
00:06:14,060 --> 00:06:18,384
Paste in the gcp-service key
from your laptop's secrets folder;

97
00:06:18,384 --> 00:06:20,375
part 2 shows how to make one.

98
00:06:20,695 --> 00:06:22,252
Never show or commit it.

99
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:25,703
Last, create the Docker network,
cheese-app-network:

100
00:06:25,703 --> 00:06:29,583
a private network where our containers
find each other by name.

101
00:06:29,903 --> 00:06:31,823
Pause and run these three steps.

102
00:06:32,967 --> 00:06:34,844
Stage 3: the containers.

103
00:06:35,164 --> 00:06:39,538
Pull the Chroma image, then stop
and remove any old vector database.

104
00:06:39,858 --> 00:06:42,934
The error,
No such container, is harmless here:

105
00:06:42,934 --> 00:06:45,618
|| true makes the step safe to run again.

106
00:06:45,867 --> 00:06:50,626
Then run it detached
on cheese-app-network, publishing port 8000,

107
00:06:50,626 --> 00:06:53,995
restarting always,
with a data folder mounted.

108
00:06:54,315 --> 00:06:56,384
Its name goes into the phone book.

109
00:06:56,750 --> 00:07:00,431
An extra check: the port is published,
the heartbeat answers,

110
00:07:00,431 --> 00:07:03,086
Chroma version one at the time of recording.

111
00:07:03,334 --> 00:07:06,150
Now the loader, run vector db cli.

112
00:07:06,470 --> 00:07:11,099
It finds the database by name,
cheese-app-vector-db, not by address.

113
00:07:11,419 --> 00:07:15,003
The project is the README's default;
change it to yours.

114
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:21,074
It downloads, creates the collection,
and loads eleven files:

115
00:07:21,074 --> 00:07:23,079
14,356 chunks in all.

116
00:07:23,399 --> 00:07:27,474
The embeddings come precomputed,
so our placeholder key never mattered.

117
00:07:27,717 --> 00:07:31,472
The loader ran with the --rm flag,
so it exits and is gone.

118
00:07:31,792 --> 00:07:34,010
Pause and run both containers now.

119
00:07:34,250 --> 00:07:35,253
A question.

120
00:07:35,573 --> 00:07:36,895
The loader is gone.

121
00:07:37,215 --> 00:07:40,970
Are the 14,356 chunks gone too?

122
00:07:50,117 --> 00:07:52,674
No. The loader only sent them;

123
00:07:52,674 --> 00:07:56,389
they sit in the vector database container,
which keeps running.

124
00:07:56,709 --> 00:07:59,226
Remove it,
and the loader must fill a new one.

125
00:07:59,467 --> 00:08:01,387
But where inside that container?

126
00:08:01,707 --> 00:08:07,552
A small slip: Chroma saves to /data,
so the folder the README mounts stays empty.

127
00:08:07,872 --> 00:08:11,665
To keep the data on the VM disk,
mount /data instead,

128
00:08:11,665 --> 00:08:15,531
rerun the stop, remove and run lines,
then the loader.

129
00:08:16,667 --> 00:08:18,608
Next, run api-service.

130
00:08:18,928 --> 00:08:23,046
The image is not on the VM yet,
so docker run pulls it first.

131
00:08:23,284 --> 00:08:26,361
Two mounts, persistent-folder and secrets;

132
00:08:26,361 --> 00:08:30,025
port 9000; the key path;
and the database by name.

133
00:08:30,345 --> 00:08:32,563
The project is the README's default,

134
00:08:32,563 --> 00:08:35,337
and the bucket name is passed
but never used.

135
00:08:38,450 --> 00:08:43,591
The logs show loading CNN model,
then uvicorn running on port 9000.

136
00:08:43,911 --> 00:08:47,112
The model comes from a GitHub release,
not a bucket,

137
00:08:47,112 --> 00:08:49,821
so our picture grays out the slide's bucket.

138
00:08:50,067 --> 00:08:53,551
And the image is big,
about five gigabytes on disk,

139
00:08:53,551 --> 00:08:55,123
with TensorFlow inside.

140
00:08:55,367 --> 00:08:57,415
Three extra curl checks:

141
00:08:57,415 --> 00:09:02,706
the root says Welcome to AC215,
and /docs shows the docs page.

142
00:09:03,026 --> 00:09:07,612
/status says 404:
the public image is older than your clone.

143
00:09:07,850 --> 00:09:10,794
The README then offers
an interactive variant.

144
00:09:11,114 --> 00:09:15,253
Run as written, it fails:
the name api-service is in use,

145
00:09:15,253 --> 00:09:18,304
because the detached container still runs.

146
00:09:18,550 --> 00:09:23,457
Remove the running one with docker rm -f,
and the variant opens a shell.

147
00:09:23,777 --> 00:09:27,553
It is optional:
we exit and start the detached one again.

148
00:09:28,850 --> 00:09:31,453
Then the frontend, on port 3000.

149
00:09:31,773 --> 00:09:37,086
The extra check shows the title, Formaggio,
and the base API URL,

150
00:09:37,086 --> 00:09:41,501
/api-service,
which the page calls on its own address.

151
00:09:41,821 --> 00:09:43,250
Pause and start both.

152
00:09:44,400 --> 00:09:46,747
Stage 4: a web server in front.

153
00:09:47,067 --> 00:09:50,691
A reverse proxy gives
the browser one address:

154
00:09:50,691 --> 00:09:55,813
nginx listens on port 80 and forwards
each request inside the VM.

155
00:09:56,050 --> 00:09:58,803
In the repository's nginx.conf,

156
00:09:58,803 --> 00:10:05,287
the location block on line 35 catches paths
that start with /api-service.

157
00:10:05,607 --> 00:10:09,255
It passes them to api-service on port 9000.

158
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:12,103
Look at the end of that address: one slash.

159
00:10:12,423 --> 00:10:17,159
With it, nginx swaps the matched prefix
for that slash,

160
00:10:17,159 --> 00:10:21,895
so /api-service/status reaches the API
as just /status.

161
00:10:22,133 --> 00:10:26,293
Everything else goes to the next block,
the frontend on 3000.

162
00:10:26,613 --> 00:10:30,219
Both targets are container names
on cheese-app-network.

163
00:10:30,467 --> 00:10:34,414
The README writes this file
with one long echo in single quotes.

164
00:10:34,734 --> 00:10:37,019
But the file holds single quotes too,

165
00:10:37,019 --> 00:10:41,219
so the shell eats the log_format
and leaves a dash and two brackets.

166
00:10:41,517 --> 00:10:44,760
Routing still works,
but the access logs are useless.

167
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:49,965
Write it with a quoted heredoc instead:
cat, the same text, then EOF.

168
00:10:50,200 --> 00:10:53,443
If you used the echo,
rewrite the file this way first.

169
00:10:53,763 --> 00:10:57,752
Then run nginx with that file mounted,
publishing port 80.

170
00:10:58,072 --> 00:11:03,960
The container list shows four:
ports 80, 3000, 9000 and 8000.

171
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:08,693
Open the external IP of your VM,
from the VM instances list,

172
00:11:08,693 --> 00:11:11,688
in a browser, and the home page appears.

173
00:11:12,008 --> 00:11:18,216
On the VM, curl agrees:
200 OK, served by nginx, title Formaggio.

174
00:11:18,450 --> 00:11:24,381
/api-service says Welcome to AC215:
the prefix was cut on the way.

175
00:11:24,701 --> 00:11:26,322
Pause and open your site.

176
00:11:26,567 --> 00:11:27,570
A question.

177
00:11:27,890 --> 00:11:30,770
Port 9000 is published on the VM.

178
00:11:31,090 --> 00:11:32,839
Does it answer from your laptop?

179
00:11:41,683 --> 00:11:47,934
No. The two ticks opened only 80 and 443;
no rule opens 9000.

180
00:11:48,254 --> 00:11:52,702
Port 9000 is open on the VM
but shut at the network edge:

181
00:11:52,702 --> 00:11:53,950
one public door.

182
00:11:54,617 --> 00:11:55,705
Now the puzzle.

183
00:11:56,025 --> 00:12:00,377
The web server looks up frontend
and api-service once, at start.

184
00:12:00,697 --> 00:12:05,104
With the frontend stopped,
the restart fails that lookup and exits,

185
00:12:05,104 --> 00:12:07,011
taking the API route with it.

186
00:12:07,250 --> 00:12:11,943
Start the frontend, then nginx,
and the site answers 200 again.

187
00:12:12,263 --> 00:12:15,509
Start order matters,
and so does a restart policy,

188
00:12:15,509 --> 00:12:18,301
which only the vector database command has.

189
00:12:19,700 --> 00:12:21,215
Let's count what we typed.

190
00:12:21,535 --> 00:12:24,959
Twenty-one commands on the VM,
plus console clicks,

191
00:12:24,959 --> 00:12:27,913
plus three Docker Hub scripts on the laptop.

192
00:12:28,233 --> 00:12:30,324
Each one added a block to the VM.

193
00:12:30,567 --> 00:12:31,932
Now three questions.

194
00:12:32,252 --> 00:12:35,964
Repeat: can you build the same thing
for a staging copy tomorrow?

195
00:12:36,284 --> 00:12:38,674
Review: what exactly is on that VM?

196
00:12:38,994 --> 00:12:41,255
Undo: how do you delete every piece?

197
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,660
The lecture answers with one idea:

198
00:12:43,660 --> 00:12:47,153
too many steps to keep track of,
so let a tool do them.

199
00:12:47,567 --> 00:12:49,402
That is infrastructure as code.

200
00:12:49,722 --> 00:12:51,272
You describe the servers,

201
00:12:51,272 --> 00:12:55,858
the networks and the software
in a real programming language, Python here,

202
00:12:55,858 --> 00:12:57,594
and keep it in Git to share.

203
00:12:57,914 --> 00:13:00,409
No YAML file, no special language.

204
00:13:00,650 --> 00:13:05,461
An earlier version of the course
did these same stages as Ansible playbooks,

205
00:13:05,461 --> 00:13:06,474
written in YAML.

206
00:13:06,794 --> 00:13:09,589
This version does them with Pulumi,
one to one.

207
00:13:09,950 --> 00:13:14,899
One more thing the README never says:
delete this VM now, before part 2.

208
00:13:15,219 --> 00:13:20,617
In the console, open VM instances,
select it, and delete it with its boot disk.

209
00:13:20,937 --> 00:13:23,838
Pause and delete yours;
the list should be empty.

210
00:13:24,083 --> 00:13:28,883
Part 2 builds it all again, as code,
and lets a tool remember every piece.

211
00:13:30,033 --> 00:13:31,033
Part 2.

212
00:13:31,270 --> 00:13:34,812
Each Pulumi program says what should exist,
not what to do.

213
00:13:35,132 --> 00:13:41,528
One firewall rule from this repository:
a constructor, compute.Firewall;

214
00:13:41,528 --> 00:13:43,926
a logical name, allow-http;

215
00:13:43,926 --> 00:13:49,788
and arguments: port 80, from anywhere,
for VMs tagged http-server.

216
00:13:50,033 --> 00:13:52,956
The lecture's bucket example
has the same shape.

217
00:13:53,276 --> 00:13:58,374
Running this line creates nothing:
the constructor only registers the resource.

218
00:13:58,616 --> 00:14:04,095
This is the lecture's picture of Pulumi:
the command line tool runs your program,

219
00:14:04,095 --> 00:14:07,747
which produces the desired state,
a tree of resources.

220
00:14:07,983 --> 00:14:12,484
Pulumi also loads its state,
what it created last time, from your bucket.

221
00:14:12,804 --> 00:14:16,836
The --refresh flag first checks
that state against what really exists.

222
00:14:17,083 --> 00:14:23,123
Then it compares desired with current,
and each difference becomes a plan line:

223
00:14:23,123 --> 00:14:27,174
+ to create, ~ to update,
- to delete, +- to replace.

224
00:14:27,416 --> 00:14:29,594
Finally it calls the providers,

225
00:14:29,594 --> 00:14:34,371
plugins for Google Cloud, Docker and more,
which make the API calls.

226
00:14:34,691 --> 00:14:36,382
The banner on the slide:

227
00:14:36,382 --> 00:14:39,832
Pulumi manages infrastructure
through cloud APIs.

228
00:14:40,066 --> 00:14:41,879
The lecture lists seven commands.

229
00:14:42,199 --> 00:14:43,920
The tutorial uses five:

230
00:14:43,920 --> 00:14:49,005
stack, config, preview, up and destroy,
with refresh only as a flag.

231
00:14:49,325 --> 00:14:53,336
The command new stays gray,
because both projects already exist.

232
00:14:53,583 --> 00:14:58,106
Now the README's command:
pulumi up --stack dev --refresh -y.

233
00:14:58,426 --> 00:15:00,516
Up previews, asks, then applies.

234
00:15:00,836 --> 00:15:02,756
Stack dev picks the environment.

235
00:15:03,076 --> 00:15:05,807
Refresh syncs the state with reality first.

236
00:15:06,050 --> 00:15:10,271
-y answers yes to the question,
do you want to perform this update,

237
00:15:10,271 --> 00:15:12,791
and skips your chance to check the plan.

238
00:15:13,111 --> 00:15:15,031
Step 3 shows when that matters.

239
00:15:15,351 --> 00:15:17,676
Preview stops before anything changes.

240
00:15:17,916 --> 00:15:18,919
A question.

241
00:15:19,239 --> 00:15:21,863
You run pulumi up again and changed nothing.

242
00:15:22,183 --> 00:15:23,570
What should the plan show?

243
00:15:32,416 --> 00:15:33,483
Nothing to do.

244
00:15:33,803 --> 00:15:38,064
Desired equals state equals reality,
so there is no difference,

245
00:15:38,064 --> 00:15:40,971
and nothing is created, changed or deleted.

246
00:15:41,291 --> 00:15:42,394
Hold that thought:

247
00:15:42,394 --> 00:15:45,152
one stack in this repository
breaks the rule.

248
00:15:46,300 --> 00:15:50,620
The lecture splits the automated deployment
into six steps:

249
00:15:50,620 --> 00:15:54,940
setup, images,
VM, provisioning, containers and web server.

250
00:15:55,260 --> 00:15:58,076
Each maps to a file; we start with step 1.

251
00:15:58,317 --> 00:16:02,647
First, enable four APIs:
Compute Engine, Service Usage,

252
00:16:02,647 --> 00:16:06,189
Cloud Resource Manager
and Artifact Registry.

253
00:16:06,509 --> 00:16:10,370
The app's chat also needs Vertex AI,
from earlier lectures.

254
00:16:10,617 --> 00:16:12,601
Then create two service accounts.

255
00:16:12,921 --> 00:16:18,048
Deployment gets six roles,
among them Compute Admin, Compute OS Login,

256
00:16:18,048 --> 00:16:21,710
Artifact Registry Administrator
and Storage Admin.

257
00:16:22,030 --> 00:16:26,019
Create a JSON key,
and rename it deployment.json.

258
00:16:26,267 --> 00:16:31,320
The second, gcp-service, gets three roles:
Storage Object Viewer,

259
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:35,440
Vertex AI Administrator
and Artifact Registry Reader.

260
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:38,896
Its key becomes gcp-service.json.

261
00:16:39,133 --> 00:16:40,133
Why two?

262
00:16:40,328 --> 00:16:41,864
Deployment builds things.

263
00:16:42,184 --> 00:16:45,226
It needs admin rights
on your group's project,

264
00:16:45,226 --> 00:16:48,797
not the README's default,
to create the VM and log in.

265
00:16:49,117 --> 00:16:51,805
The narrow account only runs things:

266
00:16:51,805 --> 00:16:55,688
it reads images
and stored files and uses Vertex AI.

267
00:16:55,933 --> 00:17:00,285
Both keys go into a secrets folder next
to your clone, not inside it.

268
00:17:00,605 --> 00:17:04,382
The deployment script of the next step
looks three folders up

269
00:17:04,382 --> 00:17:06,301
from src/deployment to find it.

270
00:17:06,621 --> 00:17:08,243
Pause and place your keys.

271
00:17:08,483 --> 00:17:10,995
One step is missing from the README:

272
00:17:10,995 --> 00:17:14,414
nothing creates
the Artifact Registry repository.

273
00:17:14,734 --> 00:17:18,603
We create it with one extra
gcloud line in the next step,

274
00:17:18,603 --> 00:17:22,947
once the deployment container runs;
skip it, and the push fails.

275
00:17:24,083 --> 00:17:27,207
Rather than each of you
installing different tools,

276
00:17:27,207 --> 00:17:31,187
the README packs them into one container,
the deployment toolbox.

277
00:17:31,507 --> 00:17:36,286
Lines 9 to 17 of docker-shell.sh set
its environment.

278
00:17:36,933 --> 00:17:40,389
Lines 34 to 43 wire it to your laptop.

279
00:17:40,709 --> 00:17:42,584
They mount the Docker socket,

280
00:17:42,584 --> 00:17:46,009
so it builds on your Docker,
then deployment as /app,

281
00:17:46,009 --> 00:17:49,776
the secrets, a Docker config
and the three service folders.

282
00:17:50,550 --> 00:17:52,619
Change into src/deployment.

283
00:17:52,939 --> 00:17:57,770
In docker-shell.sh,
set GCP_PROJECT to your project ID;

284
00:17:57,770 --> 00:17:59,702
ours is a placeholder.

285
00:17:59,950 --> 00:18:02,915
Now run docker-shell.sh, as committed.

286
00:18:03,235 --> 00:18:06,796
Downloads answer 404 Not Found,
the build stops,

287
00:18:06,796 --> 00:18:11,320
and the script runs the container anyway:
pull access denied.

288
00:18:11,566 --> 00:18:15,133
The cause is line 4:
the base image is Debian 11,

289
00:18:15,133 --> 00:18:18,990
bullseye,
whose security updates moved to an archive.

290
00:18:19,310 --> 00:18:22,596
Fix 1:
change bullseye to bookworm, Debian 12.

291
00:18:23,200 --> 00:18:24,200
Run it again.

292
00:18:24,501 --> 00:18:26,571
Now it stops at line 29:

293
00:18:26,571 --> 00:18:30,624
google-cloud-sdk
has no installation candidate.

294
00:18:30,944 --> 00:18:32,264
Google renamed it,

295
00:18:32,264 --> 00:18:36,149
and apt even names the replacement,
google-cloud-cli.

296
00:18:36,383 --> 00:18:38,644
Fix 2: rename both packages.

297
00:18:38,964 --> 00:18:42,292
Here is the real diff of the two fixes,
one line each.

298
00:18:42,612 --> 00:18:44,340
Pause and make both edits.

299
00:18:45,066 --> 00:18:49,631
The third run builds in about 92 seconds,
and the entrypoint starts.

300
00:18:49,951 --> 00:18:53,066
First error: invalid_grant:
Invalid grant: account not found.

301
00:18:53,386 --> 00:18:57,952
Our key belongs to no account;
yours activates the deployment account.

302
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:00,525
The project and registry helper pass.

303
00:19:00,845 --> 00:19:02,235
Then the state bucket:

304
00:19:02,235 --> 00:19:05,709
ours could not be created,
and Pulumi could not log in.

305
00:19:06,029 --> 00:19:10,338
Yours creates the bucket the first time,
logs in, and leaves you here.

306
00:19:10,583 --> 00:19:14,039
Check the tools: gcloud, Pulumi and kubectl.

307
00:19:14,359 --> 00:19:18,460
One surprise:
kubectl is newer than the Dockerfile pins,

308
00:19:18,460 --> 00:19:22,780
because Google's apt repository
has its own kubectl package

309
00:19:22,780 --> 00:19:25,708
with a higher version, and apt picks it.

310
00:19:25,950 --> 00:19:28,190
Last check: gcloud auth list.

311
00:19:28,510 --> 00:19:30,622
Ours says No credentialed accounts.

312
00:19:30,942 --> 00:19:34,005
Yours lists the deployment
account as active;

313
00:19:34,005 --> 00:19:36,659
if not, check the file name in secrets.

314
00:19:36,979 --> 00:19:41,395
Then create the registry repository
with the extra line from earlier.

315
00:19:41,715 --> 00:19:43,016
Pause and run both.

316
00:19:44,250 --> 00:19:46,426
This is the one setup the slides skip.

317
00:19:46,746 --> 00:19:52,015
Pulumi will log in to the VM over SSH
to run scripts and drive Docker.

318
00:19:52,335 --> 00:19:53,423
Who logs in?

319
00:19:53,743 --> 00:19:57,280
The deployment service account,
through OS Login:

320
00:19:57,280 --> 00:20:01,900
the VM accepts a key registered
to that account's login profile,

321
00:20:01,900 --> 00:20:05,221
under a Linux username
Google derives from it.

322
00:20:05,467 --> 00:20:08,240
First, turn OS Login on for the project.

323
00:20:08,560 --> 00:20:11,995
Typed as printed,
the template fails with no such file.

324
00:20:12,315 --> 00:20:16,453
It is the angle bracket lesson again:
replace the whole placeholder.

325
00:20:16,700 --> 00:20:21,073
The README example uses its own project,
which you change to yours.

326
00:20:21,393 --> 00:20:24,785
Ours says no active account;
yours prints Updated.

327
00:20:25,033 --> 00:20:31,406
Next, the key: in /secrets, run ssh-keygen
with the name ssh-key-deployment,

328
00:20:31,406 --> 00:20:34,676
press Enter twice, and go back to /app.

329
00:20:34,996 --> 00:20:37,641
No passphrase: Pulumi cannot type one.

330
00:20:37,900 --> 00:20:41,890
The two files now sit
in the secrets folder on your laptop:

331
00:20:41,890 --> 00:20:45,879
the private key, readable only by you,
and the public half.

332
00:20:46,117 --> 00:20:49,253
Then upload the public half
to the OS Login profile.

333
00:20:49,573 --> 00:20:51,559
Ours stops at the same error;

334
00:20:51,559 --> 00:20:56,080
the README shows yours,
ending in a username that starts with sa_.

335
00:20:56,400 --> 00:20:59,023
Copy it: the VM stack's config needs it.

336
00:20:59,267 --> 00:21:03,619
A small slip: the next heading asks you
to edit inventory.yml.

337
00:21:03,939 --> 00:21:05,286
No such file exists;

338
00:21:05,286 --> 00:21:09,059
it belongs to an earlier,
Ansible version of the course.

339
00:21:09,379 --> 00:21:11,405
The Pulumi config replaces it.

340
00:21:12,650 --> 00:21:14,975
Step 2: build and push the images.

341
00:21:15,295 --> 00:21:21,137
Your laptop's arrow lands on the registry;
slide 21 still calls it GCR,

342
00:21:21,137 --> 00:21:25,087
its retired name:
today it is Artifact Registry.

343
00:21:25,333 --> 00:21:29,333
Pulumi keeps environments apart
as stacks of one program,

344
00:21:29,333 --> 00:21:33,824
dev, staging and production,
each with its own state and config.

345
00:21:34,144 --> 00:21:37,109
The tutorial has one stack,
dev, per project.

346
00:21:37,350 --> 00:21:41,001
Its full name,
organization/deploy-images/dev,

347
00:21:41,001 --> 00:21:44,731
is used by the VM project,
so do not rename it.

348
00:21:45,051 --> 00:21:47,185
Every stack state sits in your bucket.

349
00:21:47,433 --> 00:21:51,294
Change into deploy_images
and run pulumi stack init dev.

350
00:21:51,614 --> 00:21:53,099
Without a bucket login,

351
00:21:53,099 --> 00:21:56,457
Pulumi offers its own cloud
and waits for a browser.

352
00:21:56,777 --> 00:22:01,172
If yours says the stack already exists,
use pulumi stack select dev.

353
00:22:01,417 --> 00:22:06,324
Config set, preview and up all print:
PULUMI_ACCESS_TOKEN must be set.

354
00:22:06,644 --> 00:22:09,625
To show them anyway, we use a local folder,

355
00:22:09,625 --> 00:22:12,745
pulumi login --local:
extra, and not for you.

356
00:22:12,983 --> 00:22:15,803
Stack init answers Created stack 'dev',

357
00:22:15,803 --> 00:22:19,853
and config set writes your project
into Pulumi.dev.yaml.

358
00:22:20,100 --> 00:22:21,273
Now the program.

359
00:22:21,593 --> 00:22:25,107
Line 13 makes a timestamp,
down to the second,

360
00:22:25,107 --> 00:22:28,697
and lines 14 and 15 build
the registry address.

361
00:22:28,933 --> 00:22:32,567
Each image is one
docker_build.Image resource:

362
00:22:32,567 --> 00:22:36,333
a timestamped tag,
push true, retain_on_delete,

363
00:22:36,333 --> 00:22:38,256
and one platform, AMD64.

364
00:22:38,576 --> 00:22:41,562
An Apple silicon Mac emulates that slowly.

365
00:22:41,882 --> 00:22:46,448
Lines 34 and 35 export the tag
for the other project.

366
00:22:46,683 --> 00:22:49,033
The extra preview, frontend only:

367
00:22:49,033 --> 00:22:53,876
five provider plugins, one image
to create with its timestamped tag,

368
00:22:53,876 --> 00:22:56,582
and a warning: Skipping preview build.

369
00:22:56,902 --> 00:22:58,395
A preview never builds.

370
00:22:58,717 --> 00:23:02,646
Up does build:
the npm install, the Next.js build,

371
00:23:02,646 --> 00:23:06,653
then the push,
which fails here with 403 Forbidden.

372
00:23:06,973 --> 00:23:11,965
Yours builds all three images, slowly,
and ends with each tag in the outputs.

373
00:23:12,285 --> 00:23:13,437
Pause and run it.

374
00:23:14,550 --> 00:23:16,257
Remember the earlier question?

375
00:23:16,577 --> 00:23:20,299
This stack breaks the rule:
each up rebuilds the images,

376
00:23:20,299 --> 00:23:22,891
because the timestamp is new every run.

377
00:23:23,211 --> 00:23:26,127
Each run can add image layers
to the registry,

378
00:23:26,127 --> 00:23:27,649
and storage costs money.

379
00:23:28,983 --> 00:23:30,860
Step 3: create the VM.

380
00:23:31,180 --> 00:23:34,081
The slide draws a network share beside it;

381
00:23:34,081 --> 00:23:38,156
in this code that is a second disk,
mounted at /mnt/disk-1.

382
00:23:38,900 --> 00:23:42,073
Change into deploy_single_vm,
then stack init,

383
00:23:42,073 --> 00:23:44,418
three config sets, preview and up:

384
00:23:44,418 --> 00:23:48,073
without the bucket login,
each prints the same error.

385
00:23:48,700 --> 00:23:50,175
On the local backend,

386
00:23:50,175 --> 00:23:53,757
the config shows the project,
your masked username,

387
00:23:53,757 --> 00:23:55,931
and the account the VM runs as:

388
00:23:55,931 --> 00:23:59,367
deployment at your own project,
not the README's.

389
00:23:59,687 --> 00:24:03,228
Pause, set all three,
and check them with pulumi config.

390
00:24:03,966 --> 00:24:07,434
The main file makes four calls,
in the lecture's order:

391
00:24:07,434 --> 00:24:11,074
create the instance,
provision it, set up the containers,

392
00:24:11,074 --> 00:24:12,478
set up the web server.

393
00:24:13,216 --> 00:24:15,840
Create instance builds the fences first.

394
00:24:16,160 --> 00:24:20,856
There is a cloud network,
also called cheese-app-network,

395
00:24:20,856 --> 00:24:22,944
and three firewall gates:

396
00:24:22,944 --> 00:24:26,870
80 and 443 for the tagged VMs,
22 for every VM.

397
00:24:27,116 --> 00:24:32,065
Then a 50 GB data disk,
and a lookup of the newest Ubuntu image.

398
00:24:32,385 --> 00:24:34,437
The VM address is an output,

399
00:24:34,437 --> 00:24:37,954
a sealed envelope
that opens once the VM exists.

400
00:24:38,200 --> 00:24:44,184
The real preview plans a disk, a network
and three firewalls, then stops at line 100,

401
00:24:44,184 --> 00:24:48,760
invalid_grant: the image lookup
is the first call needing Google.

402
00:24:49,080 --> 00:24:51,704
Yours goes on to about thirty resources.

403
00:24:51,950 --> 00:24:53,898
Check the plan before -y:

404
00:24:53,898 --> 00:24:58,883
a newer Ubuntu image can turn the next up
into a VM replacement.

405
00:24:59,203 --> 00:25:00,611
Then pause and run up.

406
00:25:00,931 --> 00:25:03,622
From here it costs
roughly two dollars a day,

407
00:25:03,622 --> 00:25:05,176
plus disks and an address.

408
00:25:05,916 --> 00:25:12,337
Step 4, provisioning, has no slide picture:
it happens inside the VM, over SSH.

409
00:25:12,657 --> 00:25:18,716
Nine remote commands run in a chain: format
and mount the disk, update, install Docker.

410
00:25:19,266 --> 00:25:22,615
Pulumi remembers that a command ran,
not what it changed.

411
00:25:22,935 --> 00:25:27,015
Look at the first one:
blkid checks for a filesystem,

412
00:25:27,015 --> 00:25:30,786
and only if there is none,
mkfs formats the disk.

413
00:25:31,033 --> 00:25:32,036
A question.

414
00:25:32,356 --> 00:25:36,388
Why check with blkid first,
instead of just formatting the disk?

415
00:25:45,233 --> 00:25:47,025
Because formatting erases.

416
00:25:47,345 --> 00:25:52,006
If this step ever ran again,
after a new trigger or a replaced resource,

417
00:25:52,006 --> 00:25:56,796
plain formatting would wipe the data disk:
the chat history and the model.

418
00:25:57,116 --> 00:26:00,870
Scripts Pulumi cannot inspect
must be safe to run twice.

419
00:26:02,117 --> 00:26:03,909
Step 5: the containers.

420
00:26:04,229 --> 00:26:07,933
The VM program asks the images stack
for its tags,

421
00:26:07,933 --> 00:26:12,378
through a stack reference
to organization/deploy-images/dev.

422
00:26:12,698 --> 00:26:15,002
So run up in deploy_images first.

423
00:26:15,750 --> 00:26:19,816
Then a Docker provider whose host is SSH,
with your key,

424
00:26:19,816 --> 00:26:25,116
so each container on the VM is a resource
Pulumi can compare and replace.

425
00:26:25,850 --> 00:26:27,996
Three containers appear:

426
00:26:27,996 --> 00:26:33,274
the frontend, the vector database on 8000,
the API on 9000.

427
00:26:33,594 --> 00:26:37,882
The slide puts the frontend on 80;
the code, on 3000.

428
00:26:39,717 --> 00:26:41,530
Step 6: the web server.

429
00:26:41,850 --> 00:26:46,309
The nginx container appears on top,
and port 80 moves onto it.

430
00:26:49,234 --> 00:26:52,775
Its config file is uploaded
with a checksum as a trigger.

431
00:26:53,095 --> 00:26:56,914
Change nginx.conf,
and only these steps run again.

432
00:26:57,650 --> 00:26:59,975
No successful pulumi up was captured.

433
00:27:00,295 --> 00:27:03,090
So here is what it builds,
drawn from the code.

434
00:27:03,410 --> 00:27:06,952
It ends on six outputs,
among them the instance_ip.

435
00:27:07,200 --> 00:27:11,296
A small slip:
the exported SSH command leaves out the key.

436
00:27:11,616 --> 00:27:15,136
Add -i /secrets/ssh-key-deployment.

437
00:27:15,884 --> 00:27:18,560
The lecture marks the job of each key:

438
00:27:18,560 --> 00:27:24,054
deployment creates the VM and logs in,
the narrow key reads images and models.

439
00:27:24,300 --> 00:27:25,303
A question.

440
00:27:25,623 --> 00:27:27,372
The running app needs one key.

441
00:27:27,692 --> 00:27:30,594
Which would you hand the VM,
and why not the other?

442
00:27:39,434 --> 00:27:45,651
The gcp-service key: it reads images
and stored objects and uses Vertex AI;

443
00:27:45,651 --> 00:27:48,885
it cannot create VMs, disks or buckets.

444
00:27:49,205 --> 00:27:53,002
Deployment can,
which a compromised VM should never hold.

445
00:27:53,250 --> 00:27:54,317
Two footnotes.

446
00:27:54,637 --> 00:27:59,312
This repository pulls the images
with your deployment credentials

447
00:27:59,312 --> 00:28:01,973
and runs the VM itself as deployment,

448
00:28:01,973 --> 00:28:05,858
capped only by scopes,
a list of what the VM may call.

449
00:28:06,178 --> 00:28:09,805
And the API calls Vertex AI
with the narrow key.

450
00:28:10,125 --> 00:28:12,386
Try the narrow one as an exercise.

451
00:28:12,634 --> 00:28:15,045
The lecture's last step: test the app.

452
00:28:15,365 --> 00:28:17,062
Open your instance_ip;

453
00:28:17,062 --> 00:28:20,612
the footer shows the API
version from /status.

454
00:28:23,684 --> 00:28:29,039
On the VM, through the console's SSH button
or the fixed SSH command,

455
00:28:29,039 --> 00:28:34,628
the README checks: list the containers,
follow each log, Ctrl+C to stop.

456
00:28:34,948 --> 00:28:38,596
Our stand-in names the database
cheese-app-vector-db.

457
00:28:40,184 --> 00:28:43,491
Exec into api-service and into nginx.

458
00:28:43,811 --> 00:28:48,352
One note: chat needs a Gemini model
Vertex AI still serves;

459
00:28:48,352 --> 00:28:53,432
change it in the API's helper files,
then run up in both projects.

460
00:28:54,667 --> 00:28:58,614
The lecture ends with a slide of its own:
don't forget to delete.

461
00:28:58,850 --> 00:29:04,333
In deploy_single_vm,
run pulumi destroy --stack dev --refresh -y.

462
00:29:04,653 --> 00:29:06,125
Pause and run yours.

463
00:29:06,445 --> 00:29:08,196
Ours shows the login error;

464
00:29:08,196 --> 00:29:11,158
on the local backend,
with nothing to delete,

465
00:29:11,158 --> 00:29:12,738
it finishes in a second.

466
00:29:12,984 --> 00:29:17,153
Destroy plays the graph backwards:
the containers over SSH,

467
00:29:17,153 --> 00:29:20,898
then the VM,
the disk, the firewalls and the network.

468
00:29:21,134 --> 00:29:26,503
Run it in deploy_single_vm;
in deploy_images it only forgets the images,

469
00:29:26,503 --> 00:29:29,859
which stay in the registry,
retain_on_delete.

470
00:29:30,100 --> 00:29:31,593
Your teardown checklist.

471
00:29:31,913 --> 00:29:36,180
One: destroy the VM stack,
and check the VM list is empty.

472
00:29:36,500 --> 00:29:39,913
Two:
delete the registry repository with gcloud.

473
00:29:40,233 --> 00:29:44,159
Three: the state bucket,
last, once no stack holds anything.

474
00:29:44,400 --> 00:29:47,237
Four: the part 1 VM, if it still runs.

475
00:29:47,557 --> 00:29:50,864
Five:
your Docker Hub images, free and optional.

476
00:29:51,184 --> 00:29:54,555
Six: when the course ends,
both service account keys.

477
00:29:54,800 --> 00:29:58,629
The recap is the lecture's six steps,
each with its file:

478
00:29:58,629 --> 00:30:02,056
docker-shell.sh,
deploy_images, create_instance.py,

479
00:30:02,056 --> 00:30:06,491
provision_instance.py,
setup_containers.py and setup_webserver.py.

480
00:30:07,750 --> 00:30:10,118
The lecture also names six principles.

481
00:30:10,438 --> 00:30:14,107
Real language,
declarative, state with previews: yes.

482
00:30:14,427 --> 00:30:16,689
Components and tests: not here.

483
00:30:17,009 --> 00:30:19,036
Many providers, but one cloud.

484
00:30:19,284 --> 00:30:21,887
One VM is a single point of failure.

485
00:30:22,207 --> 00:30:25,343
Lecture 21 automates builds and tests,

486
00:30:25,343 --> 00:30:29,386
lecture 22 moves to Kubernetes
with this toolbox,

487
00:30:29,386 --> 00:30:33,513
and lecture 23 runs pulumi up
from GitHub Actions.

488
00:30:33,750 --> 00:30:34,859
Test yourself.

489
00:30:35,179 --> 00:30:41,074
Trace your instance_ip/api-service/status:
which gate, which container,

490
00:30:41,074 --> 00:30:42,844
which location block,

491
00:30:42,844 --> 00:30:46,721
and which character
makes the API see /status?

492
00:30:46,967 --> 00:30:49,689
What three things does pulumi up compare,

493
00:30:49,689 --> 00:30:52,876
what does refresh change,
and what does -y skip?

494
00:30:53,196 --> 00:30:57,335
Name the two service accounts,
where each key sits, and what each may do.

495
00:30:57,584 --> 00:31:00,763
Which of the six steps
can Pulumi inspect afterwards,

496
00:31:00,763 --> 00:31:02,683
and which does it only remember?

497
00:31:03,003 --> 00:31:04,581
What survives destroy?

498
00:31:04,901 --> 00:31:08,080
Answers are in the exercises
and the study guide.

